WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.886
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.186 --> 00:00:08.282
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.582 --> 00:00:16.645
Unit eight: Creativity and Innovation. In this unit, you will discuss how new ideas are created and developed.

4
00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:18.825
divergent thinking.

5
00:00:19.525 --> 00:00:24.300
divergent thinking means producing many different ideas from one starting point.

6
00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.354
Brainstorming encourages divergent thinking.

7
00:00:28.054 --> 00:00:31.658
Your turn. Say: divergent thinking.

8
00:00:34.358 --> 00:00:35.603
divergent thinking.

9
00:00:36.617 --> 00:00:37.804
ideation.

10
00:00:38.504 --> 00:00:42.349
ideation means the process of forming new ideas.

11
00:00:42.649 --> 00:00:45.810
The ideation session produced fifty suggestions.

12
00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:49.615
Your turn. Say: ideation.

13
00:00:52.315 --> 00:00:53.455
ideation.

14
00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:55.939
disruptive innovation.

15
00:00:56.639 --> 00:01:02.063
disruptive innovation means a new idea that completely changes an industry.

16
00:01:02.363 --> 00:01:06.104
Ride-hailing apps were a disruptive innovation for taxis.

17
00:01:06.804 --> 00:01:09.689
Your turn. Say: disruptive innovation.

18
00:01:12.389 --> 00:01:13.831
disruptive innovation.

19
00:01:14.817 --> 00:01:16.178
lateral thinking.

20
00:01:16.878 --> 00:01:21.501
lateral thinking means solving problems in creative, unexpected ways.

21
00:01:21.801 --> 00:01:24.079
The puzzle requires lateral thinking.

22
00:01:24.779 --> 00:01:27.954
Your turn. Say: lateral thinking.

23
00:01:30.654 --> 00:01:31.980
lateral thinking.

24
00:01:32.983 --> 00:01:34.368
creative process.

25
00:01:35.068 --> 00:01:40.062
creative process means the stages of developing an original idea.

26
00:01:40.362 --> 00:01:43.186
Every artist has a different creative process.

27
00:01:43.886 --> 00:01:47.166
Your turn. Say: creative process.

28
00:01:49.866 --> 00:01:51.354
creative process.

29
00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:53.432
incubator.

30
00:01:54.132 --> 00:01:58.268
incubator means an organisation that helps new businesses grow.

31
00:01:58.568 --> 00:02:01.764
The university incubator supports ten start-ups.

32
00:02:02.464 --> 00:02:05.801
Your turn. Say: incubator.

33
00:02:08.501 --> 00:02:09.502
incubator.

34
00:02:10.517 --> 00:02:11.796
breakthrough idea.

35
00:02:12.496 --> 00:02:17.340
breakthrough idea means an idea that solves a problem in a completely new way.

36
00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.778
Mobile money was a breakthrough idea for rural banking.

37
00:02:21.478 --> 00:02:24.652
Your turn. Say: breakthrough idea.

38
00:02:27.352 --> 00:02:28.551
breakthrough idea.

39
00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:30.574
iteration.

40
00:02:31.274 --> 00:02:36.002
iteration means a repeated version of something, improved each time.

41
00:02:36.302 --> 00:02:39.335
The app went through twelve iterations before launch.

42
00:02:40.035 --> 00:02:42.943
Your turn. Say: iteration.

43
00:02:45.643 --> 00:02:46.679
iteration.

44
00:02:47.683 --> 00:02:49.892
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:50.192 --> 00:02:52.958
Ms. Sarah, where do breakthrough ideas come from?

46
00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:59.082
Usually from a long creative process, not a single moment of genius.

47
00:03:00.383 --> 00:03:02.557
So divergent thinking comes first?

48
00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:10.078
Yes. During ideation, it's essential that everyone suggest ideas freely, without judgement.

49
00:03:11.383 --> 00:03:12.280
And then?

50
00:03:13.583 --> 00:03:18.500
Then you test, fail and improve. The first iteration is rarely the best.

51
00:03:19.783 --> 00:03:25.103
Our university incubator insists that each team build a prototype within a month.

52
00:03:26.417 --> 00:03:31.913
Sensible. Were it not for constant testing, few disruptive innovations would succeed.

53
00:03:33.217 --> 00:03:36.795
Grammar focus: Subjunctive and formal structures.

54
00:03:37.095 --> 00:03:44.354
The subjunctive uses the base verb after verbs like insist, suggest and recommend, and after it is essential that.

55
00:03:44.654 --> 00:03:49.884
They insist that each team build a prototype. It is essential that everyone be heard.

56
00:03:50.184 --> 00:03:52.636
Were it not for means if it were not for.

57
00:03:52.936 --> 00:03:59.069
In the subjunctive, use the base form for all persons: I suggest that he go (not goes).

58
00:04:00.083 --> 00:04:03.583
Now read and listen. How Innovation Really Happens.

59
00:04:03.883 --> 00:04:07.798
We often imagine inventors having sudden moments of inspiration.

60
00:04:09.083 --> 00:04:15.239
In reality, most breakthrough ideas emerge from a slow, messy creative process.

61
00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:21.498
It begins with divergent thinking, when people generate as many ideas as possible.

62
00:04:22.783 --> 00:04:26.443
Experts recommend that judgement be delayed during this stage.

63
00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:33.442
The best ideas are then developed into prototypes and tested through repeated iterations.

64
00:04:34.750 --> 00:04:40.267
Many start-ups are supported by incubators that provide mentoring, space and funding.

65
00:04:41.583 --> 00:04:47.043
Some of these projects become disruptive innovations that transform whole industries.

66
00:04:48.350 --> 00:04:52.927
Were it not for persistence, however, most good ideas would never leave the drawing board.

67
00:04:54.217 --> 00:04:57.101
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:04:57.401 --> 00:05:01.525
Producing many different ideas from one starting point is blank.

69
00:05:01.825 --> 00:05:06.814
A, divergent thinking. B, iteration. C, incubator.

70
00:05:10.817 --> 00:05:14.186
The answer is A: divergent thinking.

71
00:05:15.683 --> 00:05:18.461
The teacher suggested that he blank the essay again.

72
00:05:18.761 --> 00:05:21.787
A, writes. B, write. C, wrote.

73
00:05:25.783 --> 00:05:28.085
The answer is B: write.

74
00:05:29.583 --> 00:05:32.535
It is vital that every student blank on time.

75
00:05:32.835 --> 00:05:35.594
A, is. B, be. C, was.

76
00:05:39.583 --> 00:05:41.571
The answer is B: be.

77
00:05:43.083 --> 00:05:46.708
A new idea that completely changes an industry is a blank.

78
00:05:47.008 --> 00:05:52.740
A, disruptive innovation. B, creative process. C, lateral thinking.

79
00:05:56.750 --> 00:06:00.027
The answer is A: disruptive innovation.

80
00:06:01.517 --> 00:06:05.594
According to the text, experts recommend that judgement be blank.

81
00:06:05.894 --> 00:06:10.372
A, immediate. B, delayed. C, avoided completely.

82
00:06:14.383 --> 00:06:17.103
The answer is B: delayed.

83
00:06:18.617 --> 00:06:23.997
Now it's your turn. Part one: What conditions help people become more creative?

84
00:06:29.297 --> 00:06:34.304
Part two: Describe two conditions and give an example from school or work.

85
00:06:40.604 --> 00:06:52.927
It is essential that people feel safe to fail, and it helps if teams include different backgrounds. In my university project, for example, a design student suggested an idea the engineers had never considered.

86
00:06:53.227 --> 00:06:55.366
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:06:56.350 --> 00:07:01.789
Great work today! You learned eight new words and subjunctive and formal structures.

88
00:07:02.089 --> 00:07:06.378
Today's value is creativity. Use your imagination when you play.

89
00:07:06.678 --> 00:07:09.133
See you in Unit nine. Goodbye!

90
00:07:09.433 --> 00:07:12.364
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

